Sure, I can help you with that. Here's a C++ program that capitalizes the first letter of each word in a given string while also decoding the puzzle:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
string decode(string str) {
    string decoded = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
        decoded += char(str[i] - 1);
    }
    return decoded;
}
string capitalize(string str) {
    string capitalized = "";
    bool is_new_word = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
        if (is_new_word) {
            capitalized += toupper(str[i]);
            is_new_word = false;
        } else {
            capitalized += str[i];
        }
        if (str[i] == ' ') {
            is_new_word = true;
        }
    }
    return capitalized;
}
int main() {
    string str = "udpogojvlsibvv";
    string decoded = decode(str);
    string capitalized = capitalize(decoded);
    cout << capitalized << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
The `decode` function takes a string as input and returns a decoded string where each letter is the letter that comes before it in the alphabet.
The `capitalize` function takes a string as input and returns a capitalized string where the first letter of each word is capitalized.
In the `main` function, we first decode the input string using the `decode` function. Then, we capitalize the decoded string using the `capitalize` function. Finally, we print the capitalized string to the console.
