Proportional representation is a complex electoral system that requires careful consideration and planning. However, I can provide you with some guidance on how to implement it in your PHP voting system.
First, you need to understand the basic principles of proportional representation. In this system, the number of seats a party receives is proportional to the number of votes it receives. This means that smaller parties are not excluded from representation, and minority groups have a better chance of being represented.
To implement proportional representation in your PHP voting system, you will need to modify the way votes are counted and seats are allocated. One common method is the D'Hondt method, which involves dividing the number of votes each party receives by a sequence of divisors to determine the number of seats it should receive.
Here's an example of how you could implement the D'Hondt method in PHP:
```php
// Assume $parties is an array of parties, each with a number of votes
// and $numSeats is the total number of seats to be allocated
$seats = array(); // Initialize an array to hold the number of seats each party receives
for ($i = 0; $i < $numSeats; $i++) {
    $maxIndex = 0;
    $maxValue = 0;

    // Find the party with the highest quotient
    foreach ($parties as $index => $party) {
        $quotient = $party['votes'] / ($seats[$index] + 1);
        if ($quotient > $maxValue) {
            $maxIndex = $index;
            $maxValue = $quotient;
        }
    }

    // Allocate a seat to the party with the highest quotient
    $seats[$maxIndex]++;
}
// The $seats array now contains the number of seats each party receives
