How can I implement binary search in Go to find an element x in an array of size n and return its index if it exists in the array, and -1 otherwise? Can you also provide an implementation that works for an array sorted in descending order instead of ascending order, and explain how the time complexity would be affected? 
Here's the Go code for implementing binary search in ascending order:
func binarySearch(arr []int, x int) int {
    left := 0
    right := len(arr) - 1
    for left <= right {
        mid := (left + right) / 2
        if arr[mid] == x {
            return mid
        }
        if arr[mid] < x {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1
}
To implement binary search for an array sorted in descending order, you can reverse the comparison operators in the if statements:
func binarySearchDescending(arr []int, x int) int {
    left := 0
    right := len(arr) - 1
    for left <= right {
        mid := (left + right) / 2
        if arr[mid] == x {
            return mid
        }
        if arr[mid] > x {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1
}
The time complexity of the binary search algorithm is O(log n) in both ascending and descending order.

