The formal fallacy present in the passage is the fallacy of affirming the consequent. This is a type of invalid deductive reasoning where the truth of the conclusion is assumed based on the truth of the consequent of the premise. The argument takes the following form:
\begin{aligned} 
\text{If A, then B} \\
B \\
Therefore, A
\end{aligned}
In the given passage, the argument takes the following form:
\begin{aligned} 
\text{If someone is a politician, then they are corrupt} \\
Senator Johnson is a politician \\
Therefore, Senator Johnson is corrupt
\end{aligned}
This argument is invalid because there are many politicians who are not corrupt, and the premise does not establish a necessary connection between being a politician and being corrupt.
The informal fallacy present in the passage is the fallacy of hasty generalization. This is a type of invalid inductive reasoning where a conclusion is drawn based on insufficient or unrepresentative evidence. The argument takes the following form:
\begin{aligned} 
\text{Some A have property B} \\
Therefore, all A have property B
\end{aligned}
In the given passage, the argument takes the following form:
\begin{aligned} 
\text{All politicians are corrupt} \\
Senator Johnson is a politician \\
Therefore, Senator Johnson is corrupt
\end{aligned}
This argument is invalid because it makes a generalization about all politicians based on the behavior of some politicians, which is not representative of the entire group. There are many politicians who are not corrupt, and it is unfair to assume that Senator Johnson is corrupt simply because he is a politician.

