Here is some Java code that can add two fractions together:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class FractionAdder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter the numerator and denominator of the first fraction: ");
        int num1 = sc.nextInt();
        int den1 = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.println("Enter the numerator and denominator of the second fraction: ");
        int num2 = sc.nextInt();
        int den2 = sc.nextInt();
        int den3 = lcm(den1, den2);
        int num3 = num1 * (den3 / den1) + num2 * (den3 / den2);
        System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + "/" + den1 + " and " + num2 + "/" + den2 + " is " + num3 + "/" + den3);
    }
    public static int gcd(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) {
            return a;
        } else {
            return gcd(b, a % b);
        }
    }
    public static int lcm(int a, int b) {
        return (a * b) / gcd(a, b);
    }
}
```
To add the fractions 3/4 and 2/3 using command line tools, you can compile the above code and then run it with the following command:
```
java FractionAdder
```
You will then be prompted to enter the numerator and denominator of each fraction. Enter 3 for the numerator of the first fraction, 4 for the denominator of the first fraction, 2 for the numerator of the second fraction, and 3 for the denominator of the second fraction. The program will then output the sum of the two fractions, which is 17/12.

