One algorithm that is more complex than the standard sorting algorithm is the merge sort algorithm. Merge sort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that recursively divides the input array into smaller sub-arrays, sorts them, and then merges them back together. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n log n), which is faster than the O(n^2) time complexity of the standard sorting algorithm.
Here is the code to implement the merge sort algorithm in MATLAB:
```
function sorted_items = merge_sort(items)
    n = length(items);
    if n == 1
        sorted_items = items;
    else
        mid = floor(n/2);
        left = items(1:mid);
        right = items(mid+1:end);
        left_sorted = merge_sort(left);
        right_sorted = merge_sort(right);
        sorted_items = merge(left_sorted, right_sorted);
    end
end
function merged_items = merge(left, right)
    i = 1;
    j = 1;
    merged_items = [];
    while i <= length(left) && j <= length(right)
        if left(i) < right(j)
            merged_items = [merged_items, left(i)];
            i = i + 1;
        else
            merged_items = [merged_items, right(j)];
            j = j + 1;
        end
    end
    if i <= length(left)
        merged_items = [merged_items, left(i:end)];
    end
    if j <= length(right)
        merged_items = [merged_items, right(j:end)];
    end
end
```
You can call the `merge_sort` function with your list of integers as the input argument:
```
items = [3, 7, 2, 4, 1];
sorted_items = merge_sort(items);
disp(sorted_items);
```
This will output the sorted list `[1, 2, 3, 4, 7]`.

